{"causes":["The compilation of the Poetic Edda was influenced by the Christianization of Iceland, as well as the patronage of the Icelandic aristocracy.","The creation of the manuscript was facilitated by the preservation efforts of medieval scribes, who worked to protect and transmit the poetry of the Old Norse era.","The development of Scandinavian literature and folklore was shaped by the influence of the Poetic Edda, which provided a window into the cultural practices and spiritual beliefs of the Germanic peoples."],"effects":["The Poetic Edda's influence can be seen in the subsequent development of Scandinavian literature and folklore, which continued to evolve and adapt the mythology and legends contained within.","The manuscript's contents have been widely studied and adapted in various forms of media, including films, TV shows, and video games.","The Poetic Edda's legacy can be felt in modern popular culture, demonstrating the enduring power of this medieval manuscript.","The manuscript's existence and composition have contributed to a greater understanding of the Viking Age and its cultural heritage, shaping the field of Scandinavian studies and cultural history."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"Icelandic_Aristocracy","sourceName":"Icelandic Aristocracy","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Poetic Edda","targetName":"Poetic Edda","context":"The Icelandic aristocracy played a significant role in the compilation and transmission of the Poetic Edda."},{"sourceSlug":"Medieval_Scribes","sourceName":"Medieval Scribes","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Poetic Edda","targetName":"Poetic Edda","context":"Medieval scribes worked to protect and transmit the poetry of the Old Norse era, preserving the contents of the Poetic Edda."},{"sourceSlug":"Christianization_of_Iceland","sourceName":"Christianization of Iceland","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Poetic Edda","targetName":"Poetic Edda","context":"The Christianization of Iceland had a significant impact on the compilation of the Poetic Edda."},{"sourceSlug":"Old_Norse_Era","sourceName":"Old Norse Era","verb":"CONTAINS","targetSlug":"Poetic Edda","targetName":"Poetic Edda","context":"The Poetic Edda contained the poetry of the Old Norse era."},{"sourceSlug":"Scandinavian_Literature","sourceName":"Scandinavian Literature","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Poetic Edda","targetName":"Poetic Edda","context":"The Poetic Edda had a significant influence on the development of Scandinavian literature."},{"sourceSlug":"Viking_Age","sourceName":"Viking Age","verb":"CONTAINS","targetSlug":"Poetic Edda","targetName":"Poetic Edda","context":"The Poetic Edda contains poetry from the Viking Age."},{"sourceSlug":"Germanic_Peoples","sourceName":"Germanic Peoples","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Poetic Edda","targetName":"Poetic Edda","context":"The Poetic Edda reflects the cultural practices and spiritual beliefs of the Germanic peoples."},{"sourceSlug":"Medieval_Scandinavia","sourceName":"Medieval Scandinavia","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Poetic Edda","targetName":"Poetic Edda","context":"Medieval Scandinavia was influenced by the contents of the Poetic Edda."},{"sourceSlug":"Literary_Renaissance","sourceName":"Literary Renaissance","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Poetic Edda","targetName":"Poetic Edda","context":"The Poetic Edda was influenced by the literary renaissance of the Middle Ages."}],"places":[{"name":"Iceland","role":"Birthplace"},{"name":"Medieval Iceland","role":"Setting"},{"name":"Scandinavia","role":"Influence"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Poetic Edda","type":"Reference article","slug":"poetic-edda"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q205874","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Poetic_Edda"],"tags":["literary work","Historical & Literary Texts","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"Poetic Edda contributed to the historical record of the Classical period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"The Poetic Edda (Old Norse: Edda; also known as the Elder Edda or Sæmundar Edda, though neither attribution is accurate) is a collection of Old Norse mythological and heroic poems preserved primarily in the Codex Regius manuscript (Konungsbók, 'King's Book'), a vellum codex written c. 1270 CE in Iceland, donated to the Danish King Frederick III in 1662 and now in the Árni Magnússon Institute for Icelandic Studies, Reykjavik. The Codex Regius contains 29 poems; additional poems from other manuscripts are conventionally included in editions of the Poetic Edda, bringing the total to approximately 34–40 poems depending on the edition. The poems are thought to have been composed between the 9th and 13th centuries CE, with some possibly preserving material of considerably greater antiquity.

The Poetic Edda falls into two groups: mythological poems (Völuspá, 'The Seeress's Prophecy' — a cosmological narrative from the creation of the world to Ragnarök and the new world after; Hávamál, 'Words of the High One' — wisdom poetry attributed to Odin; Vafþrúðnismál, Grímnismál, Skírnismál, Lokasenna, Þrymskviða, and others dealing with Norse gods — Odin, Thor, Freyr, Loki) and heroic poems (the Sigurd cycle — Reginsmál, Fáfnismál, Sigrdrífumál, Atlakviða, Atlamál, and others — dealing with the legend of Sigurd the dragon-slayer and the Nibelungen material that also underlies the German Nibelungenlied).

The Poetic Edda is the primary source for Norse mythology and the Norse heroic tradition — it contains the most detailed surviving accounts of Norse cosmology (the Nine Worlds, Yggdrasil, the creation, Ragnarök), Norse deities (Odin, Thor, Freyr, Freyja, Loki, Baldr), and the mythological and heroic narrative material that shaped Old Norse literature, skaldic poetry, and medieval Scandinavian culture.","subjects":["Norse Literature","Medieval Era","Old Norse","Norse Mythology","Epic Poetry","Icelandic Literature","Germanic Tradition","Mythology"],"frameworks":["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"The Poetic Edda's significance lies in its role as a window into the cultural practices and spiritual beliefs of the Germanic peoples, providing valuable insights into the Viking Age and its cultural heritage.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
